NEW_SECTION
## Versioning

The version is baked into filenames (`vUX-core-4.0.0-beta.js`) and into the absolute CDN import URLs shown in the README (`http://library.vilshub.com/lib/vUX/<version>/vUX-*.js`). Bumping a version touches filenames, the import in `vUX-core.js`, and the module registry list in `vModel.core.data.modules` (`src/helpers.js`).

## Example pages (required for every module)

Every module gets a runnable example under `examples/<module>/` — `index.html`, `main.js`, `README.md` — served from the repository root with `data-id="vUX"` and `data-library-root="../../"`.

**Design the page properly. Do not ship a bare-bones demo.** [...]

  - Commit to a look
  - Lead with the component
  - Make it interactive
  - Show the failure modes too
  - Self-contained and offline
  - Responsive
  - Comment the JS for the reader

[...] `examples/autowriter/` is the reference for this standard. [...]

(19 lines added — see the file for the full text)

## Change logging (required for every change)

REASON
Requested by the user after reviewing the AutoWriter example: module examples are to use
a real, catchy design rather than a bare demo, from now on and by default.

Placed as its own section between Versioning and Change logging, i.e. among the
conventions rather than the architecture, and titled "(required for every module)" to
match the existing "(required for every change)" so both read as obligations rather than
suggestions.

Written as a standard rather than a preference, with the reasoning included ("a component
whose demo looks unfinished reads as a component that is unfinished"), because the
instruction has to hold up in sessions that never see this conversation. The seven
bullets are the concrete, checkable parts — a look, a lead, interactivity, failure modes,
offline, responsive, commented JS — so the rule is actionable rather than a matter of
taste.

The closing paragraph names examples/autowriter/ as the reference implementation and
records explicitly that examples/modal/, examples/form/ and examples/spa/ predate the
standard. Without that, the older and plainer pages are the majority and would be copied
as the house pattern, which is exactly what this change is meant to stop. It also states
why they are plainer — they demonstrate structural behaviour, not a visual effect — so
the note is not read as a blanket condemnation of them.
